<title>USA: US ag group asks when Mexico will allow sorghum in.</title>
<headline>US ag group asks when Mexico will allow sorghum in.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Feed Grains Council, a trade group, said Friday it wanted to learn how long Mexico would interrupt shipments of U.S. sorghum.</p>
<p>&quot;The question that we're asking is...for how long&quot; said spokeswoman Donna Dunn.</p>
<p>Grain sources in Mexico City told Reuters that the government had stopped processing phytosanitary permits for the grain, effectively keeping out U.S. sorghum and encouraging use of domestic sorghum.</p>
<p>Dunn said there had been occasional interruptions in the past but they ended quickly before creating a problem.</p>
<p>&quot;The question is, do they have a time frame in mind this is going to go on&quot; or a cut-off point based on supplies or prices, Dunn said.</p>
<p>The grain sources said the move on permits was intended to reduce the size of domestic stocks. Mexico harvested a large sorghum crop this year.</p>
